TI  - Seasonal impact of waterfowl on communities of macrophytes in a shallow lake
T2  - AQUATIC BOTANY
AB  - From 2005 to 2007, we established bird-proof enclosures in a small, shallow and semi-permanent lake, lacking fish, at Brown Moss, Shropshire, UK, to investigate the effects of aquatic birds on seasonal growth of submerged and emergent macrophytes. The highest density of birds on the lake was in winter (110 individuals ha(-1)) and the lowest in summer 2005 (6 ha(-1)). Plant growth varied with season but there were significantly different (F=8.03, p<0.05, df = 1) standing crops of macrophytes between bird-proof enclosures (proportion of volume occupied, 0.47 +/- 0.04) and control treatments (0.36 +/- 0.11). Different densities of birds occurred in different areas and this was reflected in their effects. Ducks, mainly mallard (Anas platyrhynchos, Linnaeus), and teal (Anas crecca, Linnaeus), damaged plants by direct consumption, uprooting and trampling, whereas larger birds, such as mute swan (Cygnus olor, Gmelin), were able to remove Typha latifolia (Linnaeus). In summer, grazing pressure was reduced as the population of birds declined. Waterfowl caused seasonal impacts on the re-development of the water plant community. However, waterfowl herbivory had low potential to shift a macrophyte-dominated state into a phytoplankton-dominated state because aquatic plants could recover, during the growing season, when bird populations declined. (C) 2011 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.
