TY  - CPAPER
AU  - Nash, DL
ED  - Tait, L
TI  - Municipality dune vegetation production: An innovative approach
T2  - 15TH ANNUAL NATIONAL CONFERENCE ON BEACH PRESERVATION TECHNOLOGY, PROCEEDINGS
CP  - 15th Annual National Conference on Beach Preservation Technology
AB  - Continuously impacted by hurricanes from 1996 through 1999, the Town of Oak Island, North Carolina was constantly pushing sand on the beach to create frontal dunes for protection of homes and infrastructure on the oceanfront. Additionally, plans were in process to acquire a United States Army Corps of Engineers (USACE) Sea Turtle Habitat Restoration Project along a 2-mile stretch of beach on Oak Island. Native plant material was not available to revegetate these foredune projects. In 1998, the author, an agricultural extension agent with the North Carolina Cooperative Extension - Brunswick County Center, presented a plan to the Oak Island Erosion Control Committee to produce sea oats (Uniola paniculata), and other native dune species, for the town's dune revegetation projects. Adapting float system technology used in North Carolina for tobacco seedling production, the author developed a system in the Town of Oak Island's greenhouse to produce large quantities of sea oat seedlings, bitter panicum (Panicum amarum) transplants, and seashore elder (Iva imbricata) transplants. Each of these species is native to the frontal dunes along the North Carolina coast. Working cooperatively with the author, the town constructed two greenhouses for production of dune plants and to support the research of the author. Town employees carried out the daily activities in the greenhouse under the direction of the author. Sea oat seeds, along with cuttings of seashore elder and bitter panicum, were harvested from indigenous plants assuring adaptability of the transplants produced. Beginning in February 2001, the USACE began placing 2.6 million cubic yards of sand on the beach at Oak Island for the Sea Turtle Habitat Restoration Project, the only project of its kind in the world. Volunteers received training to correctly plant sea oats and other dune plants, and, by April; the town's greenhouse had produced more than 90,000 sea oats, bitter panicum, and other dune plants to stabilize the new sand dunes. The project was completed in August 2001, and more than 200,000 dune plants had been produced by the town and planted on the project, more than accomplishing the project planting specifications of 160,000 plants. All plants were produced in the Town of Oak Island's greenhouse and planted by town employees with the help of many volunteers. In addition, 100,000 more sea oats were produced to revegetate other frontal sand dune projects. By growing their own sea oats and other dune vegetation, the Town of Oak Island is able to obtain transplants at a-cost considerably lower than-market value. Also, all plants produced originate from indigenous parent material assuring adaptability to the local conditions and eliminating concerns associated with plants originating in different climate zones and ecosystems.
