TI  - Performance of Gratiana spadicea (Cassidinae) on its host and on five sympatric non hosts (Solanum: Solanaceae) in Southern Brazil
T2  - RESEARCH ON CHRYSOMELIDAE, VOL 1
AB  - The Genus Gratiana is restricted to the neotropics, and tend to be host specific. Gratiana spadicea is collected exclusively in Solanum sisymbriifolium. Due to such specificity, it was introduced as a biological control agent of this invasive weed in South Africa. In Southern Brazil, G. spadicea and S. sisymbriifolium coexist with other Solanum that are hosts for other Cassidinae, including additional species of Gratiana. In this study, we evaluated if G. spadicea is able to feed, survive, and reproduce in other five native Solanum species, sympatric to S. sisymbriifolium. In laboratory, G. spadicea was able to feed, develop, and reproduce in at least three other native Solanum species, but did not survive on two non hosts Solanum. We discuss how Solanum mechanical and chemical properties are involved in the ability of G. spadicea to exploit other hosts, and that its introduction to new regions may lead to use and/or adaptation to novel hosts, including those of economic importance.
