TI  - LIFE-HISTORY AND DISTRIBUTION OF THE AUSTRALIAN WEEVIL OXYOPS-VITIOSA (COLEOPTERA, CURCULIONIDAE), A POTENTIAL BIOLOGICAL-CONTROL AGENT FOR MELALEUCA-QUINQUENERVIA (MYRTACEAE)
T2  - ANNALS OF THE ENTOMOLOGICAL SOCIETY OF AMERICA
AB  - The melaleuca tree, Melaleuca quinquenervia S. T. Blake, has become a serious pest in Florida. One of the many herbivores of this tree in Australia, its native home, is the melaleuca leaf weevil, Oxyops vitiosa Pascoe. A search of seven major insect collections in Australia located specimens of O. vitiosa from central New South Wales to northern Queensland. During 7 yr of collecting within this region, we found this weevil at 20% of our M. quinquenervia field sites. Eggs and larvae are most abundant during the southern hemisphere winter, and adults are present throughout the year. Females survive for up to 10 mo and can oviposit > 1,000 eggs in a lifetime. In southeastern Queensland, females are in reproductive diapause from December through February and begin to oviposit in March-April when there is a flush of young foliage. In laboratory studies, we determined that there are four instars of similar duration; development from egg to adult took 43-53 d. Pupation occurs in capsules formed by prepupae in the soil, presumably beneath the host plant. Only 42% of the 28 d spent in the pupal capsule was as a pupa. Both adults and larvae feed on young shoots, which causes stunted growth of saplings and suckers. In 1992, O. vitiosa was approved as a suitable insect for introduction into quarantine in the United States for final testing.
