TY  - JOUR
AU  - Humphrey, RR
TI  - Ecology of the burroweed
T2  - ECOLOGY
AB  - Burroweeds (Aplopappus fruticosus) have increased in numbers and area on ranges of southwestern U. S. A. in recent years, occupying ground that formerly supported palatable grasses. Restoration of these burroweed infested ranges to their former productivity seemingly requires control of these undesirable weeds. An ecological study revealed the following: Seed germination occurs during the cool, moist winter months with maximum germination where a moderate amount of litter covers the ground and at 3500 ft. altitude. Comparatively few seedlings survive their 1st year. Mortality is greatest during the period immediately preceding the summer rains. Fewer seedlings and mature plants are found on areas protected from grazing where there is a good stand of grass than on grazed range where there is less grass. Root growth is normally rapid, and mature plants had taproots extending down for 12-18 feet. Insect parasites usually harm the plants only slightly. The average age of mature burroweeds is estimated at from 15 to 20 years. Blossoming usually begins late in the summer rainy period when the plants are from 18-24 months old and continues until well into the fall. Ripe seeds are being produced during most of this period.
