AU  - Zhan, TY
AU  - Zhang, SR
AU  - Hua, T
AU  - Sun, J
AU  - Zhao, WW
TI  - Spatiotemporal variations in alpine grassland quality and its response to climate change and grazing activity on the Qinghai-Tibet plateau
T2  - ECOLOGICAL FRONTIERS
AB  - The Qinghai-Tibet Plateau is one of the most sensitive regions to global climate change, making the assessment of alpine grassland quality crucial for regional ecosystem management. This study aims to reveal the variations in grassland quality at multiple scales and its responses to climate change and grazing activities. A comprehensive evaluation system for the quality of alpine meadows and alpine steppes was developed, constructing a multi-scale grassland quality index (GQI) at the plot, landscape, and regional scales. Unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) multispectral remote sensing data were integrated with field survey data, utilizing statistical analysis to model relationships across different scales. Results indicate that GQI of alpine meadow decreases from southeast to northwest across the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, while GQI of alpine steppe is higher in northern Xizang Autonomous Region and lower in other areas. Climatic factors exhibit significant spatial variability in their effects on grassland quality. Precipitation and temperature jointly influence the GQI of alpine meadows, while temperature serves as the key factor affecting alpine steppe. Grazing in the low altitude area (<4000 m) has a greater impact on GQI of alpine steppe. This study reveals the dynamics of alpine grassland ecosystems quality in relation to various climatic factors, grazing activity, and altitudinal gradients within the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau. The multi-scale approach enhances the accuracy and comprehensiveness of grassland quality assessments, providing a scientific basis for the conservation and restoration of grassland ecosystems.
