TY  - JOUR
AU  - BUFFINGT.LC
AU  - HERBEL, CH
TI  - VEGETATIONAL CHANGES ON A SEMIDESERT GRASSLAND RANGE FROM 1858 TO 1963
T2  - ECOLOGICAL MONOGRAPHS
AB  - The degree of brush encroachment and the nature of the invasion on various soil types were studied. Vegetation surveys of 1858, 1915, 1928, and 1963 formed the basis for comparisons. The study area was the Hornada Experimental Range located 23 miles north of Las Cruces. Mesquite (Prosopis juliflora) was the principal invader on sandy soil. However, all of the soils in the study ware have had mesquite present on them at some time. The area with abundant mesquite was over 66,000 acres in 1963, more than 10 times as great as in 1858. Indian activity contributed to the early spread of mesquite before the advent of domestic livestock grazing. Mesquite seed dispersal by livestock was the primary factor in the later spread of mesquite to adjacent areas. Grass cover was reduced by livestock grazing and the occurrence of periodic droughts. With the introduction of seed, mesquite plants were readily established in the sparse grass cover. Once established, the grasses could not compete with the mesquite. Creosotebush (Larrea tridentata) was present on some of the shallow soils in 1858. It was dominant in 1858 on about 600 acres; in 1963 it was dominant on over 12,000 acres. Grass cover is negligible in creosotebush-dominated types. Creosote-bush has encroached upon all vegetation types and all soil textures present in the study area. Apparently, it is the best adapted shrub of the 3 studied. Tarbush (Flourensia cernua) is the principal brush species on the heavier soils. Grass cover is slowly decreasing where tarbush is present.
