AU  - ROOM, PM
AU  - JULIEN, MH
AU  - FORNO, IW
TI  - VIGOROUS PLANTS SUFFER MOST FROM HERBIVORES - LATITUDE, NITROGEN AND BIOLOGICAL-CONTROL OF THE WEED SALVINIA-MOLESTA
T2  - OIKOS
AB  - The weevil, Cyrtobagous salviniae, and moth Samea multiplicalis, were introduced into Australia for biological control of the floating weed Salvinia molesta and formed an unusually simple plant/herbivore system. Rates of weevil and moth population increase and damage to the plant were measured in field cages at latitudes 23.degree. S, 27.degree. S and 33.degree. S in the presence and absence of increased nitrogen (N) availability. All of four weevil populations died out during winter at 33.degree. S and the  weevil is unlikely to persist where daily maximum air temperatures remain below 20.degree. C for several months. Two out of four moth populations died out in mid-summer at 23.degree. S. The moth is likely to persist throughout the range of S. molesta but with populations crashing when daily maximum air temperature exceeds 34.degree. C on several consecutive days. Greater N availability reduced moth but not weevil mortality caused by extreme temperatures. Surviving populations of both insects increased faster  on N-rich plants. Models based on earlier studies of effects of temperature and N were not very successful in predicting rates of insect development and population increase. N-rich plants grew faster and suffered less damage per insect individual but, because the insects were more numerous, N-rich plants sustained more total damage i.e. herbivore numerical response to N dominated functional response. When optimal environmental conditions for a plant and its herbivores are similar, vigorous plants are likely to sustain most damage and biological control is most likely to succeed in places where growth by the target weed is most vigorous.
