AU  - DEMOTT, WR
AU  - KERFOOT, WC
TI  - COMPETITION AMONG CLADOCERANS - NATURE OF THE INTERACTION BETWEEN BOSMINA AND DAPHNIA
T2  - ECOLOGY
AB  - Because phytoplankton succession involves pulse of short duration and a degree of unpredictability, most workers have argued against resource specialization by zooplankters. Yet, in Lake Mitchell, Vermont [USA], 2 genera of cladocerans (Bosimina and Daphnia) coexist under nearly equilibrium conditions that involve an intriguing conflict. Daphnia can depress Bosminia, but apparently cannot exclude it, even though both share substantial portions of available resources. This coexistence appears mediated by the specialized foraging mode of Bosmina, which deviates markedly from filter feeding in a way that permits efficient removal of higly edible flagellates, particularly when these items occur at low density. Evaluation of the flagellate-Bosmina association and its relationship to Bosmina-Daphnia interactions involved analysis of population responses in large-scale enclosures, in situ grazing experiments with natural and introduced resources and laboratory preference tests.
